2. We set the value of `visited[start[1]]` to `start[0]` to indicate that the starting n-tersection has been visited with a path of length `0`.
3. We loop while `visited[end[1]] == Integer.MIN_VALUE`, which means we have not reached the ending n-tersection yet.
4. Inside the loop, we iterate through all the paths between adjacent n-tersections. For each path, we check if it is possible to travel from the source n-tersection to the destination n-tersection using the current path.
5. If the destination n-tersection has not been visited or if the path length to reach it is longer than the current path length, we update `visited[destination]`.
6. If `visited[end[1]] == Integer.MIN_VALUE` after the loop, it means we have not reached the ending n-tersection, and we return `"cannot be travelled"`.
7. Otherwise, if `visited[end[1]] != Integer.MIN_VALUE`, it means we have reached the ending n-tersection, and we return `"can be travelled"`.

