public boolean canArrange(int[] arr, int k) {
    int n = arr.length;
    if (n % 2 == 1) return false;
    int[] freq = new int[k];
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        freq[arr[i] % k]++;
    }
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        freq[i] = freq[i] >> 1;
    }
    int count = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        if (freq[i] > 0) count++;
    }
    return count % 2 == 0;
}
```
This method first initializes an array `freq` to store the number of elements in `arr` that are congruent to each modulo `k`. Then, it iterates through `arr` and updates `freq[i]` for each element `i` in `arr` such that `freq[i]` is incremented by 1.

After the update, `freq[i]` stores the number of pairs that have a sum equal to `i` (modulo `k`). We can then iterate through `freq` and find the number of pairs that have a sum divisible by `k`. If there are an even number of such pairs, the method returns `true`, otherwise it returns `false`.

The time complexity of this method is O(n), where n is the length of `arr`. The space complexity is also O(n), as we need to store the entire array `freq`.

